>Why do we keep those notes but destroy the object identification
>ones?   If a PC is relying totally on his memory, he should lose that
>information as well. If he's keeping that information recorded (in a
>notebook, say), why should he record the one and not the other?

  Because, in taking notes to obviate the effects of a possible
mind flayer attack, you are trying to *circumvent* a game result
with an out-of-game process.  Much as if you used a hex editor to
modify the save file to re-identify everything and re-learn 
spells.

  The game says if your character dies without wearing an amulet
of life saving, that game is over.  Restoring a copy of a 
previous save file is circumventing the in-game result, using an
out of game process.

  Naming every item collectively might also be a way of 
legitimately circumventing the in-game result of mindflayer 
attacks.  It might not.  There's no real "in-game" explanation of
how that works.  You *can* argue that anything the game *lets* 
you do is, almost by definition, legal.  But you're not going to 
convince everyone.  (Possibly not even anyone. :^)

  (When I started playing solitaire on a computer, I had no real 
notion of move sub-stacks off of one stack to another stack.  Nor
of pulling cards off of the stacks that start aces back down to a
tableau stack.  But the game(s) let me do it and I've come to 
accept it.)


  Naming every item individually, or enough to re-identify 
everything after a mindflayer attack, argues slightly better.
There are indications that individual naming involves marking
on the item itself, ingame.  Those marks would not be erased
by a mind-flayers attack ingame, so that would be an in-game,
and possibly acceptable, method of obviating mindflayer attacks.

  The problem is that there are difficulties with the concept 
that naming an item individually involves physically marking it 
someshow.  (Let's not go into that right now, okay? ;^)

  And, again, you're not going to convince everyone.



  Now, using an athame to engrave on the floor?  Go to it.  That 
would be using a plainly in-game method of recording the 
information in a way that a mindflayer attack would not erase it 
in-game.

